## Your first connection: a device that declares its dashboard

If you write the firmware, the shortest path is to let the device describe its own dashboard. The SerialStudio device library (Arduino, C and Python, in the `firmware/` directory of the Serial Studio repository) sends one declaration line with the channel names, units, ranges and widgets, and Serial Studio builds the dashboard from it on connect.

```cpp
#include <SerialStudio.h>

SerialStudio studio(Serial, "Minimal");

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
}

void loop() {
  studio.poll();

studio.group(F("Analog"));
  studio.channel(F("A0"), analogRead(A0)).units(F("counts")).range(0, 1023);
  studio.send();

### Step 1: prepare your device

Upload this sketch to an Arduino (or adapt it for your board):

```cpp
void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  pinMode(A0, INPUT);
  pinMode(A1, INPUT);
}

void loop() {
  float t = analogRead(A0) * 0.1;
  float h = analogRead(A1) / 10.0;
  Serial.print("temp:");
  Serial.print(t);
  Serial.print(", hum:");
  Serial.println(h);
  delay(20);
}
```

The requirement is that your device prints numbers, one line per sample, ended by a newline (`\n`, `\r`, or `\r\n`). Serial Studio detects the line format from the first line: `temp:23.5, hum:60`. Names such as `temp` and `hum` title the channels.

### Custom Script

You write a `mqtt(frame)` function (in JavaScript or Lua) that receives the raw bytes of one parsed frame (the same bytes the [Frame Parser](Frame-Parser.md) script sees) and returns the payload to publish, or `nil` / `null` to skip the frame.

```javascript
// JavaScript
function mqtt(frame) {
  var parts = String(frame).split(",");
  return JSON.stringify({
    temperature: parseFloat(parts[0]),
    humidity:    parseFloat(parts[1]),
  }) + "\n";
}
```

```lua
-- Lua
function mqtt(frame)
  return string.format("temperature=%s,humidity=%s\n",
                       string.match(tostring(frame), "([^,]+),([^,]+)"))
end
```

Use this when the consumer is a CSV-friendly tool (Telegraf, an `mqtt-csv` bridge, a logging script) and you want a stream of plain values with no JSON overhead.

### Dashboard Data (JSON)

One JSON document per publish-rate tick. Unlike the older one-frame-per-publish behavior, the document is *aggregated*: each dataset's `value` and `numericValue` are **arrays** that hold every sample collected during the tick, in arrival order. A `frameCount` field at the root records how many frames were aggregated.

```json
{
  "title": "Audio Test",
  "frameCount": 3,
  "groups": [
    {
      "title": "Sensors",
      "datasets": [
        { "title": "Temperature", "value": ["21.4", "21.5", "21.5"], "numericValue": [21.4, 21.5, 21.5] },
        { "title": "Humidity",    "value": ["43",   "43",   "44"  ], "numericValue": [43, 43, 44] }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

